vents_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commit/3232dd51edd3c2fe46198614e436e6b434a9359c","node_id":"C_kwDODMcke9oAKDMyMzJkZDUxZWRkM2MyZmU0NjE5ODYxNGU0MzZlNmI0MzRhOTM1OWM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commit/60f858efbaa75e3d4757a81f9ada9d8f37502fa0","sha":"60f858efbaa75e3d4757a81f9ada9d8f37502fa0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commits/60f858efbaa75e3d4757a81f9ada9d8f37502fa0"}],"sha":"3232dd51edd3c2fe46198614e436e6b434a9359c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commits/3232dd51edd3c2fe46198614e436e6b434a9359c"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/88789928?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/saikumarrs","id":88789928,"login":"saikumarrs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg4Nzg5OTI4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commits/60f858efbaa75e3d4757a81f9ada9d8f37502fa0/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T10:25:39Z","email":"88789928+saikumarrs@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Sai Kumar Battinoju"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T10:25:39Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: stop DMT queue reclaiming XhrQueue items on storage quota error (#3212)\n\n* fix: stop DMT queue reclaiming XhrQueue items on storage quota error\n\nThe DeviceModeTransformation queue and the XhrQueue both resolved to the\nstore name `rudder_<writeKey>`. They sit on different storage engines, so\nthe collision stays invisible until a localStorage quota error moves the\nXhrQueue store into the in-memory engine and merges the two keyspaces. DMT\nthen reclaims XhrQueue items, which carry no `destinationIds`, and the\nresulting TypeError escapes as an unhandled rejection with the item wedged\nin `inProgress`.\n\n- Rename the DMT queue to `rudder_dmt`\n- Derive the quota swap''s keys through `createValidKey` and write back\n  through `set()`, keeping the durable copy for client data stores\n- Read the discovered store handle in `reclaim` rather than rebuilding it,\n  so a donor that swapped to memory stays readable\n- Report foreign items in `sendTransformedEventToDestinations` instead of\n  throwing out of the filter\n- Use two-argument `then` in `getAsyncData` so an exception from the\n  callback cannot re-enter the callback\n\n* fix(analytics-js): keep the durable copy until the in-memory write is verified\n\nThe quota swap removed every durable entry before writing any of them to\nmemory. `set` swallows serialisation, encryption and storage failures and\n`get` returns null on a parse or decryption failure, so either path could\nleave an entry with no readable copy at all.\n\nWrite each value to memory first, confirm it reads back, and only then drop\nit from the engine captured before the swap. Skip the removal entirely when\nthe store is already on the in-memory engine, so a second swap cannot delete\nwhat it just wrote.\n\nAlso drop the client data migration test: production client data stores are\ncreated without `validKeys` (`StoreManager.initClientDataStores`), so the\nconfiguration it asserted never occurs. The retention test stays, since it\npins the `noCompoundKey` branch.\n\n* fix(analytics-js-plugins): detect RetryQueue name collisions\n\n`findOtherQueues` matches donor queues on the store name alone, so two queues\nsharing a name reclaim each other''s items as soon as they land on the same\nstorage engine. That went unnoticed until it corrupted delivery.\n\nGuard it from both ends. A unit test asserts the four plugin `QUEUE_NAME`\nconstants are distinct, so a duplicate literal fails CI. `start` logs an error\nwhen another running queue already holds the name, which covers a collision\nbetween names computed at runtime. Names are claimed on `start` and released\non `stop`, since `start` calls `stop` internally when already active.\n\nRaise the `Core (Bundled) - Modern - NPM (UMD)` size limit to 42.5 KiB. The\nguard puts it 20 bytes over the old 42 KiB ceiling; 42.5 KiB is what the\nequivalent CJS bundle already allows.\n\n* fix(analytics-js-plugins): track every running queue id per name\n\nThe collision registry held one id per name, so a colliding queue that stopped\nfirst removed the only entry while the original was still running, and the next\ncolliding queue then started without an error.\n\nHold a set of ids per name and release the name only once it is empty.\n\n* fix(analytics-js): keep donor queue entries durable when storage is full\n\nTwo gaps opened up by making the quota swap actually remove the durable copy.\n\n`durableEngine.removeItem` can throw NS_ERROR_STORAGE_BUSY, and the swap runs\ninside `Store.set`''s catch, so the error escaped `set` and the write that\ntriggered the fallback never reached memory. Catch it and leave the stale entry\nfor a later cleanup instead of aborting the migration.\n\n`tryReclaim` writes its marker to the donor queue''s handle. If that write hit\nthe quota, the donor swapped to memory and dropped its durable copy; an\nabandoned handshake then left those events unreachable, since `findOtherQueues`\nonly scans the reclaiming queue''s own engine. Stores built for another queue''s\nentries now opt out of the in-memory fallback via `noSwapOnQuota`, so the\nentries stay where a later scan can still find them. The current tab''s own\nqueue keeps the fallback it has today.\n\nThe mocked Store had no quota handling at all, so it threw where the real one\nrecovers. Mirror the real contract so tests exercising this path mean something.\n\nRaise the Core (Bundled) - Modern - NPM (ESM) size limit to 42.5 KiB, matching\nthe CJS and UMD bundles it sits alongside.","tree":{"sha":"ef80b6d5a08404631dd92403d5dadcaf2ee99385","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/git/trees/ef80b6d5a08404631dd92403d5dadcaf2ee99385"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/git/commits/60f858efbaa75e3d4757a81f9ada9d8f37502fa0","verification":{"payload":"tree ef80b6d5a08404631dd92403d5dadcaf2ee99385\nparent 0f2a7efaa66d05875414d3e06260455dc118437d\nauthor Sai Kumar Battinoju <88789928+saikumarrs@users.noreply.github.com> 1789554339 +0530\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789554339 +0530\n\nfix: stop DMT queue reclaiming XhrQueue items on storage quota error (#3212)\n\n* fix: stop DMT queue reclaiming XhrQueue items on storage quota error\n\nThe DeviceModeTransformation queue and the XhrQueue both resolved to the\nstore name `rudder_<writeKey>`. They sit on different storage engines, so\nthe collision stays invisible until a localStorage quota error moves the\nXhrQueue store into the in-memory engine and merges the two keyspaces. DMT\nthen reclaims XhrQueue items, which carry no `destinationIds`, and the\nresulting TypeError escapes as an unhandled rejection with the item wedged\nin `inProgress`.\n\n- Rename the DMT queue to `rudder_dmt`\n- Derive the quota swap''s keys through `createValidKey` and write back\n  through `set()`, keeping the durable copy for client data stores\n- Read the discovered store handle in `reclaim` rather than rebuilding it,\n  so a donor that swapped to memory stays readable\n- Report foreign items in `sendTransformedEventToDestinations` instead of\n  throwing out of the filter\n- Use two-argument `then` in `getAsyncData` so an exception from the\n  callback cannot re-enter the callback\n\n* fix(analytics-js): keep the durable copy until the in-memory write is verified\n\nThe quota swap removed every durable entry before writing any of them to\nmemory. `set` swallows serialisation, encryption and storage failures and\n`get` returns null on a parse or decryption failure, so either path could\nleave an entry with no readable copy at all.\n\nWrite each value to memory first, confirm it reads back, and only then drop\nit from the engine captured before the swap. Skip the removal entirely when\nthe store is already on the in-memory engine, so a second swap cannot delete\nwhat it just wrote.\n\nAlso drop the client data migration test: production client data stores are\ncreated without `validKeys` (`StoreManager.initClientDataStores`), so the\nconfiguration it asserted never occurs. The retention test stays, since it\npins the `noCompoundKey` branch.\n\n* fix(analytics-js-plugins): detect RetryQueue name collisions\n\n`findOtherQueues` matches donor queues on the store name alone, so two queues\nsharing a name reclaim each other''s items as soon as they land on the same\nstorage engine. That went unnoticed until it corrupted delivery.\n\nGuard it from both ends. A unit test asserts the four plugin `QUEUE_NAME`\nconstants are distinct, so a duplicate literal fails CI. `start` logs an error\nwhen another running queue already holds the name, which covers a collision\nbetween names computed at runtime. Names are claimed on `start` and released\non `stop`, since `start` calls `stop` internally when already active.\n\nRaise the `Core (Bundled) - Modern - NPM (UMD)` size limit to 42.5 KiB. The\nguard puts it 20 bytes over the old 42 KiB ceiling; 42.5 KiB is what the\nequivalent CJS bundle already allows.\n\n* fix(analytics-js-plugins): track every running queue id per name\n\nThe collision registry held one id per name, so a colliding queue that stopped\nfirst removed the only entry while the original was still running, and the next\ncolliding queue then started without an error.\n\nHold a set of ids per name and release the name only once it is empty.\n\n* fix(analytics-js): keep donor queue entries durable when storage is full\n\nTwo gaps opened up by making the quota swap actually remove the durable copy.\n\n`durableEngine.removeItem` can throw NS_ERROR_STORAGE_BUSY, and the swap runs\ninside `Store.set`''s catch, so the error escaped `set` and the write that\ntriggered the fallback never reached memory. Catch it and leave the stale entry\nfor a later cleanup instead of aborting the migration.\n\n`tryReclaim` writes its marker to the donor queue''s handle. If that write hit\nthe quota, the donor swapped to memory and dropped its durable copy; an\nabandoned handshake then left those events unreachable, since `findOtherQueues`\nonly scans the reclaiming queue''s own engine. Stores built for another queue''s\nentries now opt out of the in-memory fallback via `noSwapOnQuota`, so the\nentries stay where a later scan can still find them. The current tab''s own\nqueue keeps the fallback it has today.\n\nThe mocked Store had no quota handling at all, so it threw where the real one\nrecovers. Mirror the real contract so tests exercising this path mean something.\n\nRaise the Core (Bundled) - Modern - NPM (ESM) size limit to 42.5 KiB, matching\nthe CJS and UMD bundles it sits alongside.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqm6jCRC1aQ7uu5Uhl