flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commit/728d9903e5c556143a62a5e3364f38a750745f57","node_id":"C_kwDODMcke9oAKDcyOGQ5OTAzZTVjNTU2MTQzYTYyYTVlMzM2NGYzOGE3NTA3NDVmNTc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commit/cffb90a3c13ab0277497f0246893484a55b32fb3","sha":"cffb90a3c13ab0277497f0246893484a55b32fb3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commits/cffb90a3c13ab0277497f0246893484a55b32fb3"}],"sha":"728d9903e5c556143a62a5e3364f38a750745f57","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commits/728d9903e5c556143a62a5e3364f38a750745f57"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/88789928?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/saikumarrs","id":88789928,"login":"saikumarrs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg4Nzg5OTI4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commits/cffb90a3c13ab0277497f0246893484a55b32fb3/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-03T10:27:44Z","email":"88789928+saikumarrs@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Sai Kumar Battinoju"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-03T10:27:44Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(analytics-js): use in-memory session on replay (#3182)\n\n* fix(analytics-js): use in-memory session on replay\n\nBuffered events are replayed from inside the lifecycle effect, which puts\nevery state write in one batch. The storage sync effects registered by\ninit are queued behind that batch and do not run in between, so storage\nstays behind the state for the whole replay and any read that went to\nstorage picked up the previous session.\n\nrefreshSession already force-syncs session info during the replay, so a\nplain burst of events was safe. State written directly never reached\nstorage though, so a buffered startSession(id) was silently dropped: the\nnext replayed event read the old session from the cookie and\nstartOrRenewAutoTracking overwrote the manual one. A buffered reset() or\nendSession() was lost the same way.\n\nRecord the value the sync effect last handed to storage, and prefer the\nstate whenever the signal has moved past it. Storage stays authoritative\notherwise, so a session advanced by another tab still wins, and the\npersistence check keeps session tracking off for storage type ''none'',\nwhere the state can never be ahead of a storage that holds nothing.\n\nThe server-side cookies path was already covered by the in-flight request\nguard from SDK-4207, which stays set for the whole synchronous replay.\n\n* refactor(analytics-js): type last-synced values\n\nlastSyncedValues held `any`, so assignments and comparisons against the\nsession state were unchecked. Derive the value type from SessionState so\nthe two cannot drift apart.\n\nKept as a union of the session value types rather than a per-key mapped\ntype: the only operation on these values is an identity comparison, and a\nmapped type cannot be written through a union-typed key without a cast,\nwhich would put `any` back in by another name.\n\nType the memory-storage test fixture as StorageEntries too, instead of\ncasting it to the shape of the cookie fixture, so a malformed entry is\ncaught rather than hidden.","tree":{"sha":"3e252daec95abe317ca5af1f63ac4f1e799d27e9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/git/trees/3e252daec95abe317ca5af1f63ac4f1e799d27e9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/git/commits/cffb90a3c13ab0277497f0246893484a55b32fb3","verification":{"payload":"tree 3e252daec95abe317ca5af1f63ac4f1e799d27e9\nparent 201efcbedf17ee2bd7a835aed275764644375051\nauthor Sai Kumar Battinoju <88789928+saikumarrs@users.noreply.github.com> 1788431264 +0530\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788431264 +0200\n\nfix(analytics-js): use in-memory session on replay (#3182)\n\n* fix(analytics-js): use in-memory session on replay\n\nBuffered events are replayed from inside the lifecycle effect, which puts\nevery state write in one batch. The storage sync effects registered by\ninit are queued behind that batch and do not run in between, so storage\nstays behind the state for the whole replay and any read that went to\nstorage picked up the previous session.\n\nrefreshSession already force-syncs session info during the replay, so a\nplain burst of events was safe. State written directly never reached\nstorage though, so a buffered startSession(id) was silently dropped: the\nnext replayed event read the old session from the cookie and\nstartOrRenewAutoTracking overwrote the manual one. A buffered reset() or\nendSession() was lost the same way.\n\nRecord the value the sync effect last handed to storage, and prefer the\nstate whenever the signal has moved past it. Storage stays authoritative\notherwise, so a session advanced by another tab still wins, and the\npersistence check keeps session tracking off for storage type ''none'',\nwhere the state can never be ahead of a storage that holds nothing.\n\nThe server-side cookies path was already covered by the in-flight request\nguard from SDK-4207, which stays set for the whole synchronous replay.\n\n* refactor(analytics-js): type last-synced values\n\nlastSyncedValues held `any`, so assignments and comparisons against the\nsession state were unchecked. Derive the value type from SessionState so\nthe two cannot drift apart.\n\nKept as a union of the session value types rather than a per-key mapped\ntype: the only operation on these values is an identity comparison, and a\nmapped type cannot be written through a union-typed key without a cast,\nwhich would put `any` back in by another name.\n\nType the memory-storage test fixture as StorageEntries too, instead of\ncasting it to the shape of the cookie fixture, so a malformed entry is\ncaught rather than hidden.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqmUugCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAFtAQAAoSbowC96Xgza9YN5aAOTt4\nA8/VwFyk4U05Gz0Myq5c2kqaVfy1W4bNSzkf3btRQdiyYLuAt8N4zFaTo4lxikmJ\nLYR18lJV31K+iw+8mKm+8uKNwmS5FoMHUixEsn8XuaApeL06GcqowXhxI2kiHNEz\neHSYVR15Z7bTBnw9SLQALxEYZ//DGbytvdkEwjIgvQj0puZpxUBAvfSqH12SK6cq\nxk6eJ6pRH+dAg30wixotWe54w0wV9UFsKhgnvyBBlGqcGKmJ6IoBhxD6msdDO0Sw\nNYi4imhoUh4/rWcVx51o70uQs60RLTOgGVsynGkzqjn3YZhxNqcVb4uLFNDUxrA/\noZUMVJJGt+qxu1UqbVp7NX3cGuNemJ9KrinGcra66c6WNOscYwhx2R65M7je7lpu\nEwPRh0K1xhjgyEOdFinayWHJwuJeXednXWcP9pMRGW0DVOP28vnmy9QrpOoASzSW\nlJttM6jBI4dK7KSdtH9f0r0AZY4DhXte0ltCtEOnNckkMXjegyxMK7O4Gjins8nM\nqKP/x4a05lnmfGz62m0vPwdx1QIy/zykA1VUJblBr/5aymqgDyMdjNZhJpyvbDQe\nv+o9zvoA2ytQx17cEfaUhKWLVlkW7xayJOAbo+wM+NXi6+nlaKWNV8l4AP31XOCW\nBcmfLIkMQ+whlzOJOaiS\n=bDEo\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-03T10:27:45Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commit/cffb90a3c13ab0277497f0246893484a55b32fb3","node_id":"C_kwDODMcke9oAKGNmZmI5MGEzYzEzYWIwMjc3NDk3ZjAyNDY4OTM0ODRhNTViMzJmYjM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commit/201efcbedf17ee2bd7a835aed275764644375051","sha":"201efcbedf17ee2bd7a835aed275764644375051","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commits/201efcbedf17ee2bd7a835aed275764644375051"}],"sha":"cffb90a3c13ab0277497f0246893484a55b32fb3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commits/cffb90a3c13ab0277497f0246893484a55b32fb3"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/88789928?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/saikumarrs","id":88789928,"login":"saikumarrs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg4Nzg5OTI4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/saikumarrs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/commits/201efcbedf17ee2bd7a835aed275764644375051/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-02T16:09:33Z","email":"88789928+saikumarrs@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Sai Kumar Battinoju"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-02T16:09:33Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(analytics-js): batch server-side cookie requests (#3178)\n\n* revert(analytics-js): skip no-op server-side cookie writes\n\nThis reverts commit 15e0fc47, which merged #3176.\n\nBatching subsumes the benefit. The guard skipped a request when the\ncookie already held the value being written, but batching collapses a\npage load into a single request regardless, and rl_session''s expiresAt\nchanges on every load, so there is always at least one key to write.\nSkipping the other three shrinks that request''s payload without\nremoving a request. The only configuration where it saves a request is\nsessions.autoTrack: false.\n\nAgainst that it carried two regressions:\n\n- The cookie expiry no longer slid forward. Every page load used to\n  reissue Set-Cookie with maxAge, so an active user''s rl_anonymous_id\n  was effectively immortal. Skipping means it expires one year after\n  the value was last written, and that value never changes, so a user\n  active beyond the max age loses their anonymous ID.\n- A cookie written client-side in an earlier page load was never\n  promoted back to a server-set cookie while its value was unchanged,\n  leaving it capped at 7 days by Safari ITP. Detecting that needs\n  state that survives the page load, which was out of scope.\n\nRef: SDK-5409\n\n* feat(analytics-js): batch server-side cookie requests\n\nsyncValueToStorage debounced per key and built a single-entry map, so\nevery cookie got its own POST /rsaRequest. registerEffects registers\none effect per session key and they all run on init, which is why a\npage load costs four requests where one would do. The existing\ndebounce test only exercised repeats of the same key, so this was\nnever caught.\n\nReplace the per-key timers with one shared debounce and a pending map\nof session key to cookie name. Everything that changes inside the\nwindow drains into a single request. setServerSideCookies needed no\nchanges: it already iterates the map end to end, and the payload\nalready sends cookies as an array.\n\nThe store is now resolved once at drain time rather than captured per\nkey. Every batched key is backed by cookie storage by construction, so\nthey all share the same store.\n\nRequires the data service fix from SDK-5408 to be deployed, not merely\nmerged. Without it a batched request sets only the last cookie and the\nrest fall back to client-side writes.\n\nRef: SDK-4981\n\n* feat(analytics-js): report a collapsed cookie batch\n\nA data service that predates multi-cookie support emits only the last\nSet-Cookie of a batch, and some proxies merge duplicate response\nheaders. Either way the SDK recovers the missing cookies client side,\nso the feature keeps working, but each one loses the longer lifetime a\nserver set cookie gets.\n\nToday that surfaces as one error per cookie saying it could not be\nset, with nothing pointing at the cause. Add a single error naming the\ncookies that went missing and the two things that usually explain it,\nlogged once since the cause does not change between batches.\n\nThe once-only flag lives on the server side cookies state rather than\non the manager, alongside isEnabledServerSideCookies and\ndataServiceUrl, so all the server side cookie state sits together and\nis cleared by resetState. State is serialised into error reports, so\nthe Bugsnag payload fixture gains the new field.\n\nTriggered by the response readback, not the response body. An older\ndata service returns no body at all, so a body based check would stay\nsilent for exactly the deployments at risk, and handlers built from\nthe public docs return no body either. It is diagnostic only and never\nchanges how the next batch is sent.\n\nRef: SDK-4981\n\n* fix(analytics-js): tighten the batch flush and its diagnostic test\n\nReview feedback on #3178.\n\nflushServerSideCookies is only ever called by the debounce timer, so\nit is private rather than part of the class surface, and it now drops\nthe timer id it was invoked from instead of leaving a stale one.\n\nThe once-only assertion for the collapsed batch diagnostic invoked the\nsame captured callback twice, which never produced a second batch, so\na regression resetting the flag between batches would still have\npassed. It now queues a genuinely separate batch and answers that one.\nThe client-side fallback is also stubbed out, otherwise it repopulates\nthe store and the second batch has nothing missing to report, which is\nwhat a rejected cookie attribute looks like in the wild.\n\nRef: SDK-4981\n\n* fix(analytics-js): count the batch diagnostic from what was sent\n\nReview feedback on #3178.\n\nThe batch size came from the queued session keys, but\ngetEncryptedCookieData drops a key whose value fails to encrypt, so a\ndropped key was still reported as sent.\n\nThe missing list only counted cookies that never existed and still do\nnot. A cookie that already held a value and came back untouched was\nexcluded, so a server that failed to apply it went unreported.\n\nBoth now derive from what actually happened: the size from\nencryptedCookieData, and the list from cookies the response left\nexactly as the request found them. A cookie whose value moved to\nsomething else was set by another tab or request, which is expected\nrather than a failure, so it stays out of the list.\n\nRef: SDK-4981\n\n* fix(analytics-js): draw both batch diagnostic halves from one set\n\nReview feedback on #3178.\n\nThe batch size came from encryptedCookieData but the unapplied list\ncame from the queued session keys, so a key whose value failed to\nencrypt was never sent yet could still be named as unapplied. Both now\ncome from the cookies that were actually sent.\n\nAlso adds the case where the cookies already hold the values being\nwritten, which is every repeat page load. Nothing changes there, and\nthe diagnostic must stay silent: the unapplied check only runs for a\ncookie whose stored value differs from what was expected, so an\nunchanged value never reaches it.\n\nThat does leave the diagnostic blind when a collapsed batch happens to\ncarry values identical to what is already stored, since the readback\ncannot tell an applied write from an untouched cookie.\n\nRef: SDK-4981\n\n* fix(analytics-js): do not claim the cookie fallback succeeded\n\nThe collapsed-batch message said the missing cookies \"were set client\nside instead\", but the fallback is only attempted: cb() calls\nstore.set and nothing verifies the result. It is least likely to work\nin exactly the case the same message suggests, where the browser\nrejected the cookie attributes, since a script write would be rejected\nfor the same reason.\n\nAddresses Copilot low-confidence note about logMessages.ts:108.\n\nRef: SDK-4981\n\n* fix(analytics-js): drop unknowable success count from batch error\n\nThe message asserted the server set the remaining cookies, but a cookie\nthat already holds the expected value never enters the mismatch branch, so\nit is counted as a success without evidence. Only the named cookies are\nprovably unapplied, so report those and drop the derived count.\n\n* test(analytics-js): assert exact batched key set\n\nThe test looped over whatever was batched, so an empty batch asserted\nnothing. It was empty: no session key carried a value, so syncValueToStorage\ntook the remove path for every key and the storage-type boundary was never\nexercised. Give userId, anonymousId and userTraits values and assert the\nbatch is exactly [userId].\n\nAddresses Copilot low-confidence note about the vacuous batching test.\n\n* chore(analytics-js): raise modern CJS content script limit\n\nThe collapsed-batch diagnostic takes this bundle to 42518 on CI against a\n42496 limit. Raised to 42.5 KiB rather than trimming the message: it names\nthe cookies that went missing and the two things an operator would check,\nwhich is the whole point of adding it.\n\nOnly the CJS entry moved. ESM and UMD are 42237 and 42272, still inside\n41.5 KiB, so leaving their budgets where they are.","tree":{"sha":"a9b492243f01d0ae544b9a394ca9a14e60bd9e14","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/git/trees/a9b492243f01d0ae544b9a394ca9a14e60bd9e14"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rudderlabs/rudder-sdk-js/git/commits/201efcbedf17ee2bd7a835aed275764644375051","verification":{"payload":"tree a9b492243f01d0ae544b9a394ca9a14e60bd9e14\nparent 0bb2c81186cf2bcc499b49ee1d12fc6fc7631029\nauthor Sai Kumar Battinoju <88789928+saikumarrs@users.noreply.github.com> 1788365373 +0530\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788365373 +0530\n\nfeat(analytics-js): batch server-side cookie requests (#3178)\n\n* revert(analytics-js): skip no-op server-side cookie writes\n\nThis reverts commit 15e0fc47, which merged #3176.\n\nBatching subsumes the benefit. The guard skipped a request when the\ncookie already held the value being written, but batching collapses a\npage load into a single request regardless, and rl_session''s expiresAt\nchanges on every load, so there is always at least one key to write.\nSkipping the other three shrinks that request''s payload without\nremoving a request. The only configuration where it saves a request is\nsessions.autoTrack: false.\n\nAgainst that it carried two regressions:\n\n- The cookie expiry no longer slid forward. Every page load used to\n  reissue Set-Cookie with maxAge, so an active user''s rl_anonymous_id\n  was effectively immortal. Skipping means it expires one year after\n  the value was last written, and that value never changes, so a user\n  active beyond the max age loses their anonymous ID.\n- A cookie written client-side in an earlier page load was never\n  promoted back to a server-set cookie while its value was unchanged,\n  leaving it capped at 7 days by Safari ITP. Detecting that needs\n  state that survives the page load, which was out of scope.\n\nRef: SDK-5409\n\n* feat(analytics-js): batch server-side cookie requests\n\nsyncValueToStorage debounced per key and built a single-entry map, so\nevery cookie got its own POST /rsaRequest. registerEffects registers\none effect per session key and they all run on init, which is why a\npage load costs four requests where one would do. The existing\ndebounce test only exercised repeats of the same key, so this was\nnever caught.\n\nReplace the per-key timers with one shared debounce and a pending map\nof session key to cookie name. Everything that changes inside the\nwindow drains into a single request. setServerSideCookies needed no\nchanges: it already iterates the map end to end, and the payload\nalready sends cookies as an array.\n\nThe store is now resolved once at drain time rather than captured per\nkey. Every batched key is backed by cookie storage by construction, so\nthey all share the same store.\n\nRequires the data service fix from SDK-5408 to be deployed, not merely\nmerged. Without it a batched request sets only the last cookie and the\nrest fall back to client-side writes.\n\nRef: SDK-4981\n\n* feat(analytics-js): report a collapsed cookie batch\n\nA data service that predates multi-cookie support emits only the last\nSet-Cookie of a batch, and some proxies merge duplicate response\nheaders. Either way the SDK recovers the missing cookies client side,\nso the feature keeps working, but each one loses the longer lifetime a\nserver set cookie gets.\n\nToday that surfaces as one error per cookie saying it could not be\nset, with nothing pointing at the cause. Add a single error naming the\ncookies that went missing and the two things that usually explain it,\nlogged once since the cause does not change between batches.\n\nThe once-only flag lives on the server side cookies state rather than\non the manager, alongside isEnabledServerSideCookies and\ndataServiceUrl, so all the server side cookie state sits together and\nis cleared by resetState. State is serialised into error reports, so\nthe Bugsnag payload fixture gains the new field.\n\nTriggered by the response readback, not the response body. An older\ndata service returns no body at all, so a body based check would stay\nsilent for exactly the deployments at risk, and handlers built from\nthe public docs return no body either. It is diagnostic only and never\nchanges how the next batch is sent.\n\nRef: SDK-4981\n\n* fix(analytics-js): tighten the batch flush and its diagnostic test\n\nReview feedback on #3178.\n\nflushServerSideCookies is only ever called by the debounce timer, so\nit is private rather than part of the class surface, and it now drops\nthe timer id it was invoked from instead of leaving a stale one.\n\nThe once-only assertion for the collapsed batch diagnostic invoked the\nsame captured callback twice, which never produced a second batch, so\na regression resetting the flag between batches would still have\npassed. It now queues a genuinely separate batch and answers that one.\nThe client-side fallback is also stubbed out, otherwise it repopulates\nthe store and the second batch has nothing missing to report, which is\nwhat a rejected cookie attribute looks like in the wild.\n\nRef: SDK-4981\n\n* fix(analytics-js): count the batch diagnostic from what was sent\n\nReview feedback on #3178.\n\nThe batch size came from the queued session keys, but\ngetEncryptedCookieData drops a key whose value fails to encrypt, so a\ndropped key was still reported as sent.\n\nThe missing list only counted cookies that never existed and still do\nnot. A cookie that already held a value and came back untouched was\nexcluded, so a server that failed to apply it went unreported.\n\nBoth now derive from what actually happened: the size from\nencryptedCookieData, and the list from cookies the response left\nexactly as the request found them. A cookie whose value moved to\nsomething else was set by another tab or request, which is expected\nrather than a failure, so it stays out of the list.\n\nRef: SDK-4981\n\n* fix(analytics-js): draw both batch diagnostic halves from one set\n\nReview feedback on #3178.\n\nThe batch size came from encryptedCookieData but the unapplied list\ncame from the queued session keys, so a key whose value failed to\nencrypt was never sent yet could still be named as unapplied. Both now\ncome from the cookies that were actually sent.\n\nAlso adds the case where the cookies already hold the values being\nwritten, which is every repeat page load. Nothing changes there, and\nthe diagnostic must stay silent: the unapplied check only runs for a\ncookie whose stored value differs from what was expected, so an\nunchanged value never reaches it.\n\nThat does leave the diagnostic blind when a collapsed batch happens to\ncarry values identical to what is already stored, since the readback\ncannot tell an applied write from an untouched cookie.\n\nRef: SDK-4981\n\n* fix(analytics-js): do not claim the cookie fallback succeeded\n\nThe collapsed-batch message said the missing cookies \"were set client\nside instead\", but the fallback is only attempted: cb() calls\nstore.set and nothing verifies the result. It is least likely to work\nin exactly the case the same message suggests, where the browser\nrejected the cookie attributes, since a script write would be rejected\nfor the same reason.\n\nAddresses Copilot low-confidence note about logMessages.ts:108.\n\nRef: SDK-4981\n\n* fix(analytics-js): drop